      "feedback": "**Findings**\n\n- **Category:** Approach  \n  **Severity:** Medium  \n  **Finding:** The mini-plan does not explain how \u201cDiscovering\u201d and \u201cPlanned\u201d requirement states will be derived. Making `work_completed` the only terminal source covers \u201cRecorded\u201d, but the acceptance criteria require distinguishing all four states (Discovering, Planned, Recorded, explicit NONE). Without mapping the adopted-spec metadata to those states, the implementation may miss this part.  \n  **Suggestion:** Include explicit logic: adopted-spec row exists \u2192 \u201cPlanned\u201d; no row \u2192 \u201cDiscovering\u201d, unless an explicit null marker is set for \u201cNONE\u201d. Clarify how the \u201copaque source document reference\u201d relates to these states.\n\n- **Category:** Risk  \n  **Severity:** Medium  \n  **Finding:** Switching the middle Mission card to use `MissionContext.runId` instead of the task pipeline `runId` could break other parts of the UI that still expect the pipeline identity\u2014e.g., navigation links or data lookups. The plan does not audit for upstream or downstream dependencies.  \n  **Suggestion:** Before committing, grep the client and server code for references to the old run identity and ensure they are updated, emulated, or confirmed to be unaffected. Add a test that any links from the mission card resolve correctly.\n\n- **Category:** Edge-case  \n  **Severity:** Medium  \n  **Finding:** Combining the F5c snapshot with the authoritative test gate \u201cfail-soft\u201d is described, but the plan does not specify how to handle partially missing or malformed snapshots at the aggregate level. The spec forbids greenwashing skips, malformed counts, or all-skipped runs; this demands precise aggregation rules.  \n  **Suggestion:** Define an aggregation policy: e.g., if any test in the gate lacks readable F5c evidence, the summary must show \u201cNo reliable result\u201d, and per-test degradation follows the mandatory wording from the acceptance criteria. Write a dedicated test for the mixed/partial case.\n\n- **Category:** Risk  \n  **Severity:** Low  \n  **Finding:** The plan adds focused tests but does not mention verifying that existing test suites still pass. Changing the center card identity and requirement detail rendering may silently break tests that assert on the old output.  \n  **Suggestion:** Run the full existing test suite (unit, integration, and E2E) and fix any regressions before merging.\n\n- **Category:** Security  \n  **Severity:** Low  \n  **Finding:** The \u201copaque-document-backed requirements-spec source link\u201d will be rendered in the UI. If the source is user\u2011supplied, it could be used as a phishing redirect or open-redirect vector if rendered as a raw hyperlink.  \n  **Suggestion:** Validate the link\u2019s protocol (https only, no `javascript:`), apply `rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"`, or display it as plain text when the origin is not trusted.\n\n- **Category:** Dependency  \n  **Severity:** Medium  \n  **Finding:** The client-side \u201cmirrored wire types\u201d are mentioned but not explicitly addressed. The server currently serves the needed data, but the client queries and type definitions must be extended to carry the new mission context fields (adopted-requirement metadata, document reference, F5c snapshot). Without that, the render step will be incomplete.  \n  **Suggestion:** Add a concrete step: update the client\u2019s data\u2011fetching layer (e.g., GraphQL queries, REST endpoints, TypeScript types) to include the new fields, and ensure the server serializes them correctly.\n\n**Overall assessment:** The core idea\u2014using resolved identities and existing artifacts\u2014is correct, but the mini-plan lacks detail on state derivation for requirements, test\u2011evidence combination rules, and downstream impacts. It needs these refinements to meet the acceptance criteria reliably.\n\nSHIPWRIGHT_VERDICT: revise",
